.docname {Authoring Workflows}
.docauthor {BakingRL}
.include {docs}
.doclang {en}

Current BakingRL plugin authoring uses one model: trusted v4 packages with a
JavaScript extension host, browser visuals, host-owned settings metadata, and
first-class native sidecars. Runtime API `2.1.0` also adds dependencies,
extension points, contributions, resources, and declared webviews for plugin
ecosystem chains.

## Development Loop

```sh
create-bakingrl-plugin my-plugin
cd my-plugin
npm install
npm run build
npm run validate
npm run install:local
```

When the package changes, rebuild it and run `npm run install:local` again.
The host reloads package metadata and starts extension hosts according to the
enabled package state and runtime declarations.

## Extension First

Use the extension entry for package logic:

- register commands with `context.commands.registerCommand`;
- register services with `context.services.register`;
- call other services with `context.services.call`;
- persist package state with `context.storage`;
- publish package state with `context.bus.emit` and `context.registry.set`;
- discover active plugins and contributions with `context.plugins` and
  `context.extensions`;
- read declared package resources with `context.resources`;
- start or call native helpers with `context.sidecars`.

The manifest `contributes.services` array is descriptive. The implementation is
registered from `activate(context)`.

## Visuals

Use visuals for editor and overlay rectangles. A visual should fill its assigned
box, react to `update(context)`, and use `context.item.width`,
`context.item.height`, `context.item.opacity`, and `context.settings` instead
of fixed viewport assumptions.

Visuals can subscribe to host events and call services through `VisualContext`.
They should remain browser-compatible and avoid importing extension-host-only
modules.

## Browser Surfaces

Use `contributes.visuals` for reusable browser entries mounted by host-owned
surfaces such as overlays and pages. Keep the browser entry small and
communicate through the webview bridge.

Use `contributes.webviews` for package tools, settings panels, and other
host-opened browser windows that are not reusable overlay visuals.

Host-owned settings should use JSON Schema through `contributes.settings`.
Custom configuration UI can be exposed by setting `contributes.settings.ui` to
the id of a `contributes.visuals` entry whose `kind` is `config`.

## Extension Points And Resources

Use `contributes.extensionPoints` when a plugin is a platform for other
plugins. Use `contributes.contributions` when a package contributes to another
plugin's extension point. External contribution targets require a top-level
`dependencies` entry; self-targeted contributions use the local extension point
without adding a self dependency.

Use `create-bakingrl-plugin my-platform --template platform-plugin` when the
package should own the extension point, and
`create-bakingrl-plugin my-contributor --template contributor-plugin` when the
package should contribute to an existing platform.

Use `contributes.resources` for package-local content such as images, presets,
or JSON data. Public resources need a `type`; private resources remain owned by
the declaring package unless the host allows another package to read them.

For resource-only packages, use
`create-bakingrl-plugin my-content --template content-pack-plugin`. The
generated package has no runtime and keeps content outside the host and outside
platform package source.

## Services

Services are ordinary methods registered from the extension host:

```ts
context.services.register("matchStats", {
  snapshot() {
    return currentSnapshot;
  },
  reset() {
    currentSnapshot = createEmptySnapshot();
    return currentSnapshot;
  }
});
```

Declare service names and method names in `contributes.services` so the host,
developer tools, and marketplace can inspect the package before activation.

## Sidecars

Use sidecars for native Rust code, long-running helpers, or integrations that
should not run inside the Tauri process. Declare them in `runtime.sidecars` and
call them from `context.sidecars`.

Sidecars speak JSON-RPC over stdio. BakingRL supervises start, stop, restart,
logs, diagnostics, health checks, and crash-loop detection.

## Diagnostics

Use `context.logger` for runtime logs and `context.diagnostics` for user-visible
plugin diagnostics. A package should make failures obvious enough that the host
Developer UI can explain which plugin broke and why.
